Subject: register bundles: register-keyed pc2line and frame ip

What this does:
  Rebuilds a register-compiled function's pc2line table keyed by register exec
  offset (`regvm_build_pc2line`), captures the executing instruction pointer in
  `JSStackFrame.regvm_ip` at every call, construct, apply and unwind boundary,
  and teaches `build_backtrace` to read it (`regvm_frame_pc`, wired into both
  the lazy and the eager stack-frame paths). `regvm_encode_and_verify` now
  always returns the regvm-offset -> stack-pc table, and `js_create_function`
  consumes it (rebuild, then free). Bumps `REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION` 14 -> 15.

Why we need it:
  A register frame reports a register exec offset, but the compiler installs a
  pc2line table keyed by stack pc, so every register frame printed `(missing)`.
  With the table rebuilt for register offsets and the frame's ip captured at
  each boundary, a register build names the same file:line:col as the stack
  build. The table is not serialized: it is recomputed at load, so bundles
  still contain stack code and only the debug section's meaning changes (hence
  the version bump).

Needs a bytecode version bump:
  Yes. `REGVM_FORMAT_VERSION` 14 -> 15: the debug section is now interpreted as
  register-keyed.

Notes:
  Bundle plan 4.3 case 1 (non-tail `g(){ var r = f(1); return r; }`): source,
  eager bundle and lazy bundle all print
    f (t.js:3:10), g (t.js:5:23), <eval> (t.js:6:14)
  which is exactly the stack build.
  Case 2 (tail `g(){ return f(1); }`): source, eager and lazy all print
    f (t.js:3:10), <eval> (t.js:6:14)
  (no `g`; the stack build prints `g`. R2's frame elision, accepted as D12.)
  G3: box2d `fixed.js`, register build, total instructions 21,820,379,308
  (before) -> 21,885,527,528 (+0.30%, within the +0.3% target). Run-only
  (check.js, total minus a NOEVAL load): +0.26%.
  Gates: box2d checksum 546.1008391043414; test262 50/81192; diff-suite
  400/0/0; run-gates.sh p0tree p0 full 8 pass / 0 fail.

